{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"da84af66feb049c5","type":"text","text":"\n## **12:20** -\tWhat is a *gerund*? \n## **12:40** -\tWhat is To + Infinitive,\n## **12:50** - *Exercises 29/30/31*\n\n## **13:05** -\tWrap-up and homework reminder","x":-125,"y":-30,"width":649,"height":212,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"573a53e07c08edfe","type":"text","text":"# **Today's Class: Gerunds and To + Infinitive**","x":-103,"y":-110,"width":606,"height":60,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"52fd4e62ae48ac65","x":823,"y":648,"width":534,"height":425,"color":"2","type":"text","text":"## Sometimes we use two Verbs\n\n> I **love playing** football.  \n> I **hope to play** football tomorrow.\n\n\nIn sentence 1:\n**love + playing**\n\nIn sentence 2:\n**hope + to play**\n\n> In English, when we put two verbs together, the second verb sometimes changes.\n\nIt can look like:\n**verb + -ing**\n\nor:\n**to + verb**"},
		{"id":"50089554e64a096a","x":-125,"y":205,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"1","type":"text","text":"**Study Grammar 2: Gerunds and *to + infinitive*.**"},
		{"id":"f7017dd59a9a08a4","x":1428,"y":-140,"width":488,"height":375,"color":"5","type":"text","text":"**What is a Noun** \nA **noun is a naming word**. It gives a name to a person, place, thing, or idea.\n\n- **person:** teacher, mother, student\n- **place:** school, Amsterdam, beach\n- **thing:** phone, table, book\n- **idea/feeling:** love, happiness, fear\n\nIf you can put “a”, “an”, or “the” in front of it, it is often **a noun**.\n\n**Noun = name of something**  \n**Verb = what someone or something does**"},
		{"id":"d2cdae4477c5c893","x":823,"y":-140,"width":534,"height":375,"color":"5","type":"text","text":"**What is a verb** \nWhat is happening in each sentence? What is the person doing?\n\nI **play** football.  \nI **eat** pizza.  \nI **sleep** at night.  \nI **watch** Netflix.\n\n- **run** \n- **house** \n- **swim** \n- **holiday** \n- **talk**"},
		{"id":"0c3e5fc868620de0","x":3255,"y":645,"width":457,"height":1140,"color":"2","type":"text","text":"### Verbs where the meaning changes\n\nThese are important later because **gerund** and **to + infinitive** do not mean the same thing.\n\n**stop**\n\n> He stopped **smoking**.  \n> He no longer smokes.\n\n> He stopped **to smoke**.  \n> He stopped another activity because he wanted to smoke.\n\n**remember**\n\n> I remember **meeting** her.  \n> I have a memory of it.\n\n> Remember **to call** her.  \n> Do not forget to do it.\n\n**forget**\n\n> I'll never forget **visiting** New York.  \n> I remember the experience.\n\n> I forgot **to call** him.  \n> I did not do it.\n\n**try**\n\n> Try **restarting** the computer.  \n> Experiment with this solution.\n\n> Try **to restart** the computer.  \n> Make an effort to do it.\n\n**regret**\n\n> I regret **saying** that.  \n> I am sorry about something I already did.\n\n> I regret **to inform** you...  \n> I am sorry about something I am going to say now."},
		{"id":"e43180f4586e127b","x":1428,"y":645,"width":548,"height":917,"color":"2","type":"text","text":"# verb + -ing\n\n> I love **playing** football.  \n> I like **watching** films.  \n> I dislike **waiting**.  \n> I miss **seeing** my friends.  \n> I avoid **doing** homework late at night.\n\n**playing, watching, waiting, seeing, doing**\n\n> What do all these words have at the end?\n\n### After these words:\n\n**like**  \n**dislike**  \n**love**  \n**miss**  \n**avoid**\n\n### use:\n\n**verb + -ing**\n\nSo:\n\n> love + play = love **playing**  \n> miss + see = miss **seeing**  \n> avoid + make = avoid **making**\n\n> ***Love, like, dislike, miss, avoid: add -ing.***"},
		{"id":"0427a478534d55cf","x":3255,"y":-404,"width":457,"height":961,"color":"2","type":"text","text":"### Verbs commonly followed by a gerund\n\nThese are the most useful ones to learn as:\n\n**verb + verb-ing**\n\n|Verb|Example|\n|---|---|\n|admit|He admitted **cheating**.|\n|advise|I advise **taking** the train.|\n|avoid|She avoids **driving** at night.|\n|consider|We considered **moving** house.|\n|delay|They delayed **leaving**.|\n|deny|He denied **breaking** the window.|\n|dislike|I dislike **waiting**.|\n|enjoy|She enjoys **reading**.|\n|finish|He finished **doing** his homework.|\n|imagine|Imagine **living** there.|\n|involve|The job involves **travelling**.|\n|keep|Keep **trying**.|\n|mention|She mentioned **seeing** him.|\n|mind|Do you mind **opening** the window?|\n|miss|I miss **playing** football.|\n|postpone|We postponed **meeting** them.|\n|practise|You should practise **speaking** English.|\n|quit|He quit **smoking**.|\n|recommend|I recommend **visiting** London.|\n|resist|She couldn't resist **laughing**.|\n|risk|You risk **losing** everything.|\n|suggest|He suggested **going** home.|\n|tolerate|I can't tolerate **waiting** that long.|"},
		{"id":"7a1714c16abc4e7a","x":3255,"y":1836,"width":457,"height":450,"color":"2","type":"text","text":"### Verbs that can take a gerund or _to + infinitive_\n\nThese can often use **both**:\n\n|Verb|Gerund|To + infinitive|\n|---|---|---|\n|like|I like **swimming**.|I like **to swim**.|\n|love|I love **travelling**.|I love **to travel**.|\n|hate|I hate **waiting**.|I hate **to wait**.|\n|prefer|I prefer **walking**.|I prefer **to walk**.|\n|begin|It began **raining**.|It began **to rain**.|\n|start|She started **crying**.|She started **to cry**.|\n|continue|He continued **talking**.|He continued **to talk**.|"},
		{"id":"5a189c8146a08e28","x":1428,"y":1595,"width":392,"height":236,"type":"text","text":"> **Swimming is fun.**\n\nNormally **swim** is a verb because it is an action.\n\nBut in:\n\n> **Swimming is fun.**\n\nthe word **swimming** is acting like the name of an activity. That is why we call it a **gerund**."},
		{"id":"fbdec6c20d5f628a","x":2330,"y":645,"width":574,"height":742,"color":"2","type":"text","text":"## Spot the pattern\n\n**Which one sounds correct?**\n\n> I love play football.  \n> I love playing football.\n----\n\n> She avoids make mistakes.  \n> She avoids making mistakes.\n\n-----\n> We miss see our friends.  \n> We miss seeing our friends.\n\n----\n\n> The **-ing form** used like this is called a **gerund**.\n\n> You mainly need to remember **verb + -ing**. The grammar name is _gerund_."},
		{"id":"7482874a7f05e39d","x":2330,"y":1443,"width":574,"height":843,"color":"2","type":"text","text":"# To + verb\n\n> I hope **to win**.  \n> She refused **to help**.  \n> We expect **to arrive** at six.  \n> He asked me **to wait**.\n\n\n**to win**  \n**to help**  \n**to arrive**  \n**to wait**\n\n\n> What do you see before the second verb?\n\n### After these words:\n\n**hope**  \n**ask**  \n**refuse**  \n**expect**\n\n### use:\n**to + verb**\n\nFor example:\n> hope + go = hope **to go**  \n> refuse + help = refuse **to help**  \n> expect + win = expect **to win**\n\n\n\n> ***Hope, ask, refuse, expect: use to + verb.***"},
		{"id":"50a525286caecdab","x":2330,"y":2511,"width":464,"height":496,"type":"text","text":"| First Verb | Second Verb   |\n| ---------- | ------------- |\n| like       | **-ing**      |\n| dislike    | **-ing**      |\n| love       | **-ing**      |\n| miss       | **-ing**      |\n| avoid      | **-ing**      |\n| hope       | **to + verb** |\n| ask        | **to + verb** |\n| refuse     | **to + verb** |\n| expect     | **to + verb** |\n> **Look at the first verb. The first verb tells you what happens to the second verb.**\n\n"}
	],
